    "title": "What to Know About Electric Cars When Gas Prices Are Surging",
    "text": "AdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENTWar in Iran has disrupted global oil supplies, which is prompting some car shoppers to look for ways to climb off “the gas-price roller coaster.”Listen · 8:16 min A pre-owned electric vehicle dealership in North Hampton, N.H. There are exceptionally good deals to be had on used battery-powered cars.Credit...Tristan Spinski for The New York TimesMarch 21, 2026Electric car owners may be feeling smug because they don’t need to worry about soaring gasoline prices. And people with combustion engine cars may be wondering whether it’s time to buy an electric vehicle.It’s anyone’s guess how long war with Iran will last and how high oil prices will climb. But the argument for electric vehicles has clearly become stronger.Whether electric vehicles are a good option for any individual or family depends on many factors, including where you live, whether you have access to a charger and how much you drive.In the United States, prices for new electric vehicles have fallen but still average $6,500 more than vehicles that run on fossil fuels, according to Cox Automotive. From a purely financial point of view, an electric vehicle makes sense for people who will save that much on fuel and maintenance during the time they own it.The New York Times offers a tool to help people make that calculation based on local electricity prices and driving habits. But there is more to the decision than dollars and cents. Some benefits of electric vehicles are hard to put a price on, like the peace of mind that comes from not being at the mercy of geopolitics.There are tentative signs that “people want to be taken off the gas-price roller coaster,” said Jessica Caldwell, head of insights at Edmunds. The share of people researching electric vehicles on Edmunds’s online car shopping site rose to 24 percent at the beginning of March from 21 percent in early February.Subscribe to The Times to read as many articles as you like.Jack Ewing covers the auto industry for The Times, with an emphasis on electric vehicles.A version of this article appears in print on March 23, 2026, Section B, Page 3 of the New York edition with the headline: ‘Gas-Price Roller Coaster’ Increases Appeal of E.V.s. Order Reprints | Today’s Paper | SubscribeAdvertisementSKIP ADVERTISEMENT",
